Package: recoverR
Type: Package
Title: Calibrated Layer Attribution and Spatial Recoverability for Bulk
    Multi-Omics
Version: 0.1.3
Authors@R: person("Lingzhang", "Meng", email = "lzmeng@gxams.org.cn",
    role = c("aut", "cre"))
Description: Two questions that existing multi-omics tooling does not answer.
    First, does adding an omics layer actually improve OUT-OF-SAMPLE prediction
    of a given endpoint, as opposed to merely explaining variance within a
    fitted model? 'variancePartition' attributes variance to named covariates
    and 'MOFA2' attributes variance to latent factors; neither provides a
    predictive, null-calibrated attribution, and neither reports when an added
    layer is actively harmful. Second, can a BULK profile carry a
    spatially-defined phenotype at all, and with what calibrated confidence?
    Reliability layers now exist for spatial deconvolution output, but no tool
    asks the inverse question, so users are given confident archetype calls
    where the underlying data cannot support them. This package supplies
    exact Shapley decomposition of out-of-sample performance with a
    dimension-matched permutation null, split and Mondrian conformal prediction
    with abstention for bulk-to-spatial archetype inference, a sampling-
    adequacy analysis that turns tissue-extent and panel-size constraints into
    a design rule, and a validation harness that compares bulk calls against
    multiplex-immunofluorescence ground truth. Publication figures are produced
    with every plot written as both a vector PDF and a JPEG.
